INSERT INTO story_translations (story_id, lang, title, summary, html) VALUES ('2026-07-17-luxembourg-b2b-draft-law', 'en', 'Luxembourg approves draft law extending mandatory e-invoicing to domestic B2B', 'Luxembourg''s Council of Government approved a draft law on 17 July 2026 that would extend the country''s existing B2G e-invoicing mandate to domestic B2B transactions, phased in via a Peppol four-corner network from 2028 to 2029 — though it still requires parliamentary approval.', '<h3>🇱🇺 Luxembourg approves draft law extending mandatory e-invoicing to domestic B2B</h3>
<p>On 17 July 2026, Luxembourg''s Council of Government, chaired by Prime Minister Luc Frieden, approved a draft law that would extend mandatory structured e-invoicing beyond public procurement (B2G) to domestic business-to-business transactions between companies established in Luxembourg. The bill transposes Article 1 of Council Directive (EU) 2025/516 — part of the EU''s VAT in the Digital Age (ViDA) reform package.</p>
<p>The proposed rollout is phased by obligation type and company size: all businesses would need to be able to <strong>receive</strong> structured e-invoices from 1 January 2028; large and medium-sized businesses would need to start <strong>issuing</strong> them from 1 July 2028; and the remaining businesses, including SMEs, would need to issue them from 1 January 2029. Invoices would move over the same Peppol four-corner network Luxembourg already uses for B2G, structured according to EN 16931.</p>
<p>This builds directly on Luxembourg''s existing B2G mandate, which has been fully in force since March 2023 after a phase-in by company size that began in 2022. There''s no domestic real-time e-reporting layer alongside this proposal — the only e-reporting obligation on the horizon is the separate, EU-wide cross-border DRR under ViDA from 1 July 2030, covered by a companion bill.</p>
<p><strong>What this means for your team:</strong> this is still a draft, not law — it needs parliamentary approval before any 2028 date is fixed. But if you trade domestically B2B in Luxembourg, the direction of travel is now clear, and it will run on the same Peppol infrastructure as the existing B2G mandate. If you''re already B2G-compliant, getting ready for the proposed B2B rules should be a smaller lift than starting from scratch.</p>');
